\f1\fs22 Cracker was in misery. Since the news of her pregnancy, Sarah had dropped off the radar, keeping her head down, responding to no correspondence. He knew she did not want his ident on her comms records, but had not seen nor heard from Sarah and had lost track of how long it had been. Just over a week maybe? A week, without seeing her? Intolerable. He had more than once demanded the non-existent god to end his pain. He had completed a full 6 hour session staring at the wall, the very wall that she had also gazed upon, wallowing in self-pity, and thought to take his sorry carcass on a pub crawl. He never thought that drinking alone was a good idea and had no reason to think that this escapade would end well, but not even he could have guessed what would be the consequences of this little night on the turps.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good afternoon sir, would you like a seat at the bar or a table?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thanks Elvine, I would love a seat at the bar.\'94 Cracker beamed saccharine.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Elvina.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My apologies Elvina.\'94 Cracker smiled again, hoping this would be the last time for the day.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You are forgiven, darling. Now just you follow me.\'94 Elvina exposed all her perfect white teeth in a disingenuous smile that screamed \'91Give me a big tip.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He sat on a stool at the bar and gazed upon the seriously impaired occupants of the other stools, but none looked up from their drinks. A couple of Elvi had just finished a set and were hanging around the bar scamming free drinks when they zeroed in on Cracker. He had hardly gulped down his fifth drink when they sat either side of him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Great set fellas.\'94 He had not seen the set, but Cracker knew that was the only way to start a conversation with musicians who have just walked off stage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you, thank you very much.\'94 Elvis 1 said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You look like you have a lot on your mind. Do you want a couple of Elvi to listen? We are very good listeners.\'94 Elvis 2 said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sit down fellas and I\'92ll buy you both a drink.\'94 Cracker didn\'92t mind being taken for a bit of a ride if he could unload some misery. \'93Just don\'92t write a song about it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We are always looking for new inspiration.\'94 Elvis 2 said looking intensely at his brand new shoes. \'93Sometimes songs come real easy, other times the screen remains blank.\'94 He had desperately been trying to construct viable lyrics for a song about his new shoes. They were the most comfortable he had ever owned, the supple suede a tasteful dark blue, but he was at his wit\'92s end. \cb3 \'93Take for example these shoes. I love them, they are so comfortable and supple. Just look at that blue suede. I had them specially imported in from Tel Aviv, but I can\'92t seem to write a song.\'94\cb1 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Jew made shoes?\'94 Cracker slurred back at him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, I don\'92t think so.\'94 Elvis 2 looked at his clone and almost imperceptibly rolled his eyes, just a little and just enough for only his buddy to notice.\

